// Generated by scripts/ai-prompt.mjs from the scoring engine and the English methodology texts — do not edit.
export const ENGINE = "2.1.0";
export const METHODOLOGY = "TeraStockX methodology (scoring engine 2.1.0)\n\nData: every US-listed company (Nasdaq, NYSE, NYSE American, Cboe) from its SEC filings: audited annual reports (10-K, 20-F, 40-F), quarterly reports (10-Q), current reports (8-K) and insider filings (Form 4); prices from Nasdaq (delayed about 15 minutes, updated several times an hour during the US session). TTM = trailing twelve months.\n\nScores: Every company is mapped into the same data template from its audited annual reports (10-K / 20-F) and latest quarterly reports (10-Q). Each metric is converted to a 0–100 score; each dimension is the weighted average of its metrics. 100 is best — except for Risk, where lower is better. Scaling is linear between the value that scores 0 points and the value that scores 100 points, and capped outside that range. Within an area, missing metrics are skipped and the remaining weights are renormalized; an area is scored only when metrics carrying at least half of its weight are available. Data completeness is the share of the TeraScore, by weight, that rests on data.\nGrades: 80–100 excellent, 65–79 strong, 45–64 moderate, 25–44 weak, below 25 poor (for Risk the scale is reversed: a low risk score is good).\n\nTeraScore: Weighted average of the nine fundamental areas (engine 2.1). The weights were refined on the backtest below: the areas that better told apart later winners and losers, and later losses, weigh more (consistency, valuation, cash flow); those that did less weigh less (liquidity, balance sheet – which keeps a 30% share in the risk score). On each company page, “What lifts and what lowers the TeraScore?” shows every area's contribution: 50 + Σ weight × (score − 50).\nWeights: Profitability 15%, Cash-flow quality 14%, Balance-sheet strength 10%, Liquidity 3%, Growth (last fiscal year) 7%, Long-term growth 10%, Consistency 17%, Capital allocation 8%, Valuation 16%.\n\nAreas and their metrics (general profile; for each metric: the value that scores 0 points → the value that scores 100 points, and its weight inside the area):\n- Profitability – How efficiently does the company turn revenue and capital into profit?: Gross margin 10% → 60% (15%); Operating margin 0% → 30% (25%); Net margin -5% → 20% (15%); Return on invested capital (ROIC) 0% → 20% (20%); Return on equity (ROE) 0% → 25% (15%); Return on assets (ROA) 0% → 15% (10%)\n- Cash-flow quality – Are reported earnings backed by cash?: FCF margin -5% → 20% (35%); FCF conversion 30% → 110% (25%); Accrual ratio 10% → -5% (20%); Stock-based compensation / revenue 15% → 1% (20%)\n- Balance-sheet strength – How much leverage does the company carry relative to its earnings and equity?: Debt / equity 2.5× → 0.3× (20%); Net debt / EBITDA 4× → 0× (25%); Interest coverage 1.5× → 15× (20%); Altman Z''-score 1.1 → 4 (20%); Equity ratio 10% → 60% (15%)\n- Liquidity – Can short-term obligations be met from short-term assets?: Current ratio 0.6× → 2× (40%); Quick ratio 0.4× → 1.5× (35%); Cash ratio 0.05× → 0.8× (25%)\n- Growth (last fiscal year) – Year-over-year momentum in sales, earnings and cash flow.: Revenue growth (YoY) -10% → 20% (25%); Operating income growth (YoY) -25% → 30% (20%); EPS growth (YoY) -25% → 30% (35%); FCF growth (YoY) -25% → 30% (20%)\n- Long-term growth – Compound growth over up to five years.: Revenue CAGR -5% → 15% (25%); EPS CAGR -10% → 20% (35%); FCF CAGR -10% → 20% (15%); Book value per share CAGR -5% → 15% (10%); Years with revenue growth 0% → 100% (15%)\n- Consistency – How predictable and resilient are the results?: Profitable years 0% → 100% (25%); Years with positive FCF 0% → 100% (20%); Revenue-growth volatility 30% → 3% (20%); Margin volatility 10 percentage points → 1 percentage points (15%); Piotroski F-score 2 of 9 → 8 of 9 (20%)\n- Capital allocation – How well is capital returned to shareholders and reinvested?: Share-count change (annual) 5% → -3% (35%); Dividend payout (of FCF) 120% → 60% (20%); Shareholder yield 0% → 6% (20%); ROIC change -10 percentage points → 10 percentage points (25%)\n- Valuation – How expensive is the stock relative to its fundamentals?: Price / earnings (P/E) 45× → 10× (25%); EV / EBIT 40× → 8× (20%); FCF yield 0% → 8% (25%); Price / sales (P/S) 15× → 1× (10%); PEG ratio 3 → 0.8 (20%)\n- Market risk: Volatility (annualized) 70% → 15% (60%); Maximum drawdown (5 yrs) -80% → -20% (40%)\n\nRelative scoring: Margins, returns, capital structure, liquidity and valuation multiples depend strongly on the industry. For these metrics the score is 50% absolute (the thresholds above) and 50% relative: the company's position between the 10th and 90th percentile of established sector peers (market cap ≥ $300M, revenue ≥ $50M, at least 20 peers). The sector median is shown next to each metric. Growth, consistency and risk metrics use absolute thresholds only.\nMargins and returns on capital (ROIC, ROE, ROA) are scored on 60% of the latest year and 40% of the average of the two years before it, so one exceptional year does not swing the score. The company page shows this 3-year basis under the latest value.\nIf free cash flow is negative (the company burns cash), liquidity can score at most what the cash runway justifies: how many years the liquid assets cover the current burn (0.75 years or less = 0 points, 3 years or more = 100).\n\nAdapted profiles: Banks, insurers and brokers (financial profile) and REITs (real-estate profile) are scored with adapted metrics and thresholds, shown below where they differ.\n- Financial profile (banks, insurers, brokers):\n  - Profitability: Net margin 0% → 30% (25%); Return on equity (ROE) 2% → 16% (45%); Return on assets (ROA) 0.2% → 1.5% (30%)\n  - Cash-flow quality: not scored for this profile\n  - Balance-sheet strength: Equity ratio 4% → 14% (60%); Debt / equity 4× → 0.5× (40%)\n  - Liquidity: not scored for this profile\n  - Growth (last fiscal year): Revenue growth (YoY) -10% → 15% (45%); EPS growth (YoY) -25% → 25% (55%)\n  - Long-term growth: Revenue CAGR -5% → 12% (30%); EPS CAGR -10% → 15% (30%); Book value per share CAGR -5% → 12% (25%); Years with revenue growth 0% → 100% (15%)\n  - Consistency: Profitable years 0% → 100% (45%); Revenue-growth volatility 30% → 3% (30%); Margin volatility 15 percentage points → 2 percentage points (25%)\n  - Capital allocation: Share-count change (annual) 5% → -3% (50%); Shareholder yield 0% → 7% (50%)\n  - Valuation: Price / earnings (P/E) 25× → 8× (50%); Price / book (P/B) 3× → 0.8× (30%); PEG ratio 3 → 0.8 (20%)\n- Real-estate profile (REITs):\n  - Balance-sheet strength: Debt / equity 4× → 0.8× (25%); Net debt / EBITDA 10× → 4× (35%); Interest coverage 1.2× → 6× (25%); Equity ratio 15% → 55% (15%)\n  - Liquidity: not scored for this profile\n  - Valuation: Price / FCF 35× → 10× (40%); Price / book (P/B) 3× → 0.8× (30%); EV / EBITDA 30× → 10× (30%)\n\nRisk: Risk = 100 − safety score. Safety is 65% fundamental (balance sheet 30%, consistency 30%, cash flow 20%, profitability 15%, liquidity 5%) and 35% the share price's volatility and largest fall (when price history is available). Warning flags raise risk (10 points per critical and 4 per other warning) on a saturating curve: each point closes a share of the remaining gap to 100, so the riskiest companies still differ from each other.\nMarket risk metrics: Market risk: Volatility (annualized) 70% → 15% (60%); Maximum drawdown (5 yrs) -80% → -20% (40%)\n\nPartial analysis: Partial analysis. When a company's filings lack the data for an area, that area counts in the TeraScore as a neutral 50, or as the average of the company's other areas if that is lower – so missing data can never lift the score (in the backtest, companies with gaps did worse than complete ones with the same score). Figures that are undefined rather than missing – growth from a zero or negative base, ROIC with negative invested capital, the payout ratio of a company that pays no dividend – are left out of the area instead. Data completeness is the share of the TeraScore (by weight) that rests on data. Below 80%, lists show “◐ Partial · 65%” between the company's name and its TeraScore; the company page shows the percentage whenever it is below 100% and names the missing areas. With fewer than 5 scorable areas there is no TeraScore (“Little data”); companies without an annual report yet are shown as “No analysis” or “New listing”.\nEstimated figures: Estimated figures. When a company reports no operating-income line, operating income (EBIT) is approximated as pretax income plus interest expense (not for banks and insurers). A company that has reported no borrowing and no interest expense in its last two fiscal years and whose non-current liabilities are below 15% of its assets is treated as debt-free. The company page lists such estimates; the raw data shows the figures as reported. Some companies report capital expenditure only under their own XBRL element, which the SEC's standard data does not carry; then it is estimated from the company's own capex-to-revenue ratio in the nearest years that report both, or else from depreciation and amortisation.\n\nFinancial distress: Points: Altman Z'' distress zone +3 / grey zone +1; negative equity without positive FCF +2; negative FCF +1; net loss +1; interest coverage < 1.5× +2; net debt without positive EBITDA +1; equity ratio < 5% (financials) +2. 2–3 points = elevated, 4+ = high.\nTrend: 70% long-term growth + 30% last-year growth, adjusted by up to ±5 points for the operating-margin trend. ≥ 65 improving, 40–64 stable, < 40 deteriorating. Describes the past; not a forecast.\nInvestment styles: Quality: profitability ≥ 70, consistency ≥ 70, balance sheet ≥ 60, no critical flag. Growth: long-term growth ≥ 70. Value: valuation ≥ 65. Income: dividend yield ≥ 2% covered by FCF. Speculative: critical flag or losses in most years.\nSector rank: Each score is also ranked against all analysed companies in the same sector (percentile).\nMajor events: Material events are taken from SEC Form 8-K filings of the last 12 months, keeping only high-impact item types. Bankruptcy (1.03) and restatement (4.02) are critical flags; debt acceleration (2.04), delisting notice (3.01) and auditor change (4.01) are warnings, and all of them raise the risk and distress scores.\nInsider transactions: From the SEC's quarterly Form 4 data sets for the last 12 months. Only open-market purchases and sales count. Purchases by at least two insiders totalling $100,000 or more are a strength; sales outside 10b5-1 plans above 1% of market cap are a warning.\nDividend safety: Dividend safety rates how likely the current dividend is to be maintained, from 0 to 100: 75 or more is safe, 55–74 moderate, 35–54 at risk, below 35 unsafe. Components without data are left out and the remaining weights are rescaled. Components: Free-cash-flow coverage 20%, Earnings coverage 25%, Leverage 10%, Track record 20%, Earnings stability 10%, Share-price stability 15%.\nTeraScore Changes: TeraScore Changes scores each company as of every fiscal year end with only the information available then: annual reports up to that year, 8-K events of the preceding 12 months and the share price at that date (market cap estimated from today's market cap, the price change and the share-count change). TTM figures, insider data and current market data are not used for past years, and today's sector benchmarks are applied to all years. In addition, every Monday update stores a snapshot of the current TeraScore; 4- and 13-week changes compare against the latest snapshot at least that old.\nSimilar companies: Similar companies are taken from the same sector. Each candidate's distance is the difference in log market cap, plus a penalty equal to a 4.5× size gap when it belongs to a different industry, so companies of the same industry and similar size come first, while the largest companies are matched with the largest of their sector. Only scored companies are included, and one share class per company.\nCurrencies: Foreign companies listed in the US (Form 20-F / 40-F, IFRS) are analysed in their reporting currency; ratios are currency-neutral. For valuation, the US-dollar market cap is converted at the ECB reference rate (other sources for currencies the ECB does not publish). Foreign private issuers do not file quarterly XBRL reports, so their valuation uses the last fiscal year.\nQuarterly data: Quarterly figures cover the last 12 quarters from 10-Q and 10-K filings. Flows are three-month amounts: reported directly, or derived from year-to-date figures; the fourth quarter is the annual amount minus the first nine months. Per-share figures and share counts are shown only when reported for the three months, adjusted for later stock splits. Balance-sheet items are as of the quarter end.\nLimits: Scores rely solely on reported financials and market prices. They do not capture management quality, competitive position, news or guidance. Not investment advice.\n\nRed flags and strengths the site can show:\n- Negative shareholders' equity\n- Persistent losses\n- Net loss in the latest year\n- Negative free cash flow\n- Current ratio below 1.0\n- Insufficient interest coverage\n- Weak interest coverage\n- Altman Z'' in distress zone\n- Altman Z'' in grey zone\n- Net debt without positive EBITDA\n- High leverage\n- Significant revenue decline\n- Heavy shareholder dilution\n- High stock-based compensation\n- Low earnings quality\n- Dividend not covered by FCF\n- High goodwill & intangibles\n- Outdated financial data\n- Deep share-price drawdown\n- Net cash position\n- High operating margin\n- High return on invested capital\n- Profitable in every year analyzed\n- Strong long-term growth\n- Net share repurchases\n- Altman Z'' in safe zone\n- Strong cash conversion\n- Bankruptcy or receivership filing\n- Financial statements restated\n- Debt obligation accelerated\n- Delisting notice\n- Change of auditor\n- Insider buying\n- Heavy insider selling\n\nGlossary of metrics:\n- Cash runway: Years the liquid assets (cash, or current assets less inventory and receivables) would fund the current negative free cash flow. Caps the liquidity score: 0.75 years or less = 0 points, 3 years or more = 100.\n- Gross margin: Gross profit / revenue.\n- Operating margin: Operating income (EBIT) / revenue.\n- Net margin: Net income / revenue.\n- FCF margin: Free cash flow (operating cash flow − capital expenditure) / revenue.\n- Return on equity (ROE): Net income / average shareholders' equity. Not scored when equity is negative.\n- Return on assets (ROA): Net income / average total assets.\n- Return on invested capital (ROIC): NOPAT (EBIT × (1 − effective tax rate)) / average invested capital (equity + debt − cash).\n- FCF conversion: Free cash flow / net income. With a net loss, positive FCF scores 60 points.\n- Accrual ratio: (Net income − operating cash flow) / average total assets. High values indicate earnings not backed by cash.\n- Stock-based compensation / revenue: Non-cash compensation that dilutes shareholders.\n- Capex intensity: Capital expenditure / revenue.\n- Current ratio: Current assets / current liabilities.\n- Quick ratio: (Current assets − inventory) / current liabilities.\n- Cash ratio: Cash and equivalents / current liabilities.\n- Debt / equity: Financial debt / shareholders' equity. Negative equity scores 0.\n- Equity ratio: Shareholders' equity / total assets.\n- Liabilities / assets: Total liabilities / total assets.\n- Net debt: Financial debt − cash. Negative = net cash.\n- Net debt / EBITDA: Years of EBITDA required to repay net debt. Net cash scores 100 when EBITDA is positive and 50 when it is not (the cash is being used up); net debt with negative EBITDA scores 0.\n- Interest coverage: EBIT / interest expense. No interest expense scores 100.\n- Goodwill & intangibles / assets: Share of assets from acquisitions; at risk of impairment.\n- Dividend payout (of FCF): Dividends paid / free cash flow. Not scored if no dividend is paid.\n- Book value per share: Shareholders' equity / shares outstanding.\n- Altman Z''-score: 6.56·(working capital/assets) + 3.26·(retained earnings/assets) + 6.72·(EBIT/assets) + 1.05·(equity/liabilities). < 1.1 distress, > 2.6 safe.\n- Piotroski F-score: Nine binary tests on profitability, leverage, liquidity and operating efficiency (0–9).\n- Revenue growth (YoY): Change versus the prior fiscal year.\n- Operating income growth (YoY): Change versus the prior fiscal year; undefined from a negative base.\n- Net income growth (YoY): Change versus the prior fiscal year; undefined from a negative base.\n- EPS growth (YoY): Diluted earnings per share versus the prior year. 0 points if the latest year is a loss.\n- FCF growth (YoY): Free cash flow versus the prior year.\n- Revenue CAGR: Compound annual growth rate over up to five years.\n- EPS CAGR: Compound annual growth of diluted EPS over up to five years.\n- FCF CAGR: Compound annual growth of free cash flow over up to five years.\n- Book value per share CAGR: Compound annual growth of equity per share.\n- Years with revenue growth: Share of years in the window with higher revenue than the year before.\n- Operating-margin change: Change in operating margin from the first to the last year of the window (percentage points).\n- ROIC change: Change in ROIC from the first to the last year of the window (percentage points).\n- Profitable years: Share of years in the window with positive net income.\n- Years with positive FCF: Share of years in the window with positive free cash flow.\n- Revenue-growth volatility: Standard deviation of annual revenue growth.\n- Margin volatility: Standard deviation of the operating margin (net margin for financials).\n- Share-count change (annual): Positive = dilution, negative = buybacks.\n- Market capitalization: Share price × shares outstanding.\n- Enterprise value: Market cap + financial debt − cash.\n- Price / earnings (P/E): Market cap / net income (TTM). Losses score 0.\n- Price / sales (P/S): Market cap / revenue (TTM).\n- Price / book (P/B): Market cap / shareholders' equity.\n- EV / EBIT: Enterprise value / operating income (TTM).\n- EV / EBITDA: Enterprise value / EBITDA (TTM).\n- FCF yield: Free cash flow (TTM) / market cap.\n- Price / FCF: Market cap / free cash flow (TTM).\n- PEG ratio: P/E divided by EPS CAGR (in %). Negative growth or losses score 0.\n- Dividend yield: Dividends paid (TTM) / market cap.\n- Shareholder yield: (Dividends + share buybacks, TTM) / market cap.\n- Share price, 1 year: Total return of the adjusted share price over 12 months.\n- Share price CAGR, 5 years: Annualized adjusted share-price return.\n- Volatility (annualized): Standard deviation of monthly log returns over 36 months × √12.\n- Maximum drawdown (5 yrs): Largest peak-to-trough decline of the share price.\n";
